# What the API image's `COPY . .` must not pick up.
#
# **`.env` is the line that matters.** `f2c/settings.py` calls
# `load_dotenv(BASE_DIR / '.env')` at import, so a `.env` inside the image is
# not inert -- it is configuration, read on every start, and the one on a
# developer's machine holds real SMTP passwords and real storage keys. Baking it
# into an image that is pushed to a registry publishes them. Deployed
# configuration is environment variables set by Container Apps, and the image
# ships no `.env` at all.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# 123 MB of Windows CPython that a Linux image cannot run. The image builds its
# own at /venv, outside /app, in the build stage.
.venv/
venv/

# 530 MB belonging to the frontend images, which are built from `frontend/` and
# have their own ignore file. Django serves JSON; none of this reaches it.
frontend/

# The SQLite fallback, and this deployment does not use it. Shipping it would
# also ship whatever a developer happened to be testing with.
db.sqlite3
*.sqlite3

# Uploads and collectstatic output. Both are produced at runtime and belong to
# the environment, not to the artefact.
media/
staticfiles/

.git/
.github/
.gitignore
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.coverage
htmlcov/

# Editor and tooling state.
.idea/
.vscode/
.claude/

# Documentation. Read by people, never by the application.
README.md
design/
*.ps1
